The `memcpy` copies `pkt->pay.len` bytes to `icmp + 1` in the TX buffer. The TX buffer is only 1540 bytes. The `pkt->pay.len` is derived from the incoming packet's raw length. If the incoming ICMP packet has a large payload (up to the max packet size of 1540 bytes), then `pkt->pay.len` could be up to ~1500 bytes. The destination is `icmp + 1` which is at offset `sizeof(eth) + sizeof(ip) + sizeof(icmp)` = 14 + 20 + 4 = 38 bytes into the TX buffer. So copying ~1500 bytes to offset 38 would overflow the 1540-byte TX buffer.

